Biographies of Zhang Shizhi and Feng Tang

Zhang Shizhi, the Tingwei, was a man of Duyang. His courtesy name was Ji. He had an elder brother, Zhong, with whom he lived together. He became a Cavalry Gentleman (Qilang) based on his family’s wealth and served Emperor Xiaowen. For ten years he received no promotion and was unknown. Shizhi said, “Serving for a long time has diminished Zhong’s property, and I have not succeeded.” He wished to resign and return home. The General of the Middle Lang (Zhonglang Jiang), Yuan Ang, knew him to be worthy and regretted his departure, so he requested that Shizhi be transferred to fill the post of Yeshi (Auditor). After Shizhi had completed his audience, he took the opportunity to speak on matters of advantage. Emperor Xiaowen said, “Keep it low; do not make high theories that cannot be implemented now.” Thereupon Shizhi spoke for a long time about matters between the Qin and Han dynasties, explaining why Qin lost and why Han rose. Emperor Xiaowen praised him as good and appointed Shizhi as Yeshi Puye (Chief Auditor).

Shizhi accompanied the Emperor on a tour and climbed the Tiger Enclosure. The Emperor asked the Wei of Shanglin about the registers of the various birds and beasts. He asked more than ten questions, but the Wei looked left and right and could not answer any of them. The Keeper of the Tiger Enclosure, standing by, answered the Emperor’s questions about the registers of birds and beasts in great detail, wishing to show that he could respond to questions with quick, endless verbal agility. Emperor Xiaowen said, “Should not officials be like this? The Wei is useless!” He then ordered Shizhi to appoint the Keeper as the Ling (Commandant) of Shanglin. Shizhi stepped forward and said, “Your Majesty, what kind of man is the Marquis of Jiang, Zhou Bo?” The Emperor said, “A worthy man.” He then asked again, “What kind of man is the Marquis of Dongyang, Zhang Xiang?” The Emperor again said, “A worthy man.” Shizhi said, “The Marquis of Jiang and the Marquis of Dongyang are called worthy men, yet these two cannot even get words out of their mouths. How could they learn from this Keeper, who is glib and quick-witted! Moreover, Qin relied on clerks skilled in writing and law. The clerks competed with each other in speed, harshness, and scrutiny to appear superior. However, the result was merely formal appearance, without genuine compassion. Therefore, their faults were not heard, and the dynasty declined until the Second Emperor, when the empire collapsed. Now, if Your Majesty promotes the Keeper because of his verbal skill, I fear the whole world will follow the wind, competing for verbal skill without substance. Moreover, the lower imitate the upper as quickly as a shadow follows a form; actions must be carefully considered.” Emperor Xiaowen said, “Good.” He then stopped and did not appoint the Keeper.

The Emperor got into his carriage, summoned Shizhi to ride with him, and drove slowly. He asked Shizhi about the faults of Qin. Shizhi answered fully and plainly. When they reached the palace, the Emperor appointed Shizhi as the Ling of the Gongche (Imperial Carriage Office).

Shortly after, the Crown Prince and the Prince of Liang entered the court together in one carriage and did not dismount at the Sima Gate. Shizhi chased them and stopped the Crown Prince and the Prince of Liang, forbidding them from entering the palace gate. He then indicted them for not dismounting at the public gate, which was disrespectful, and submitted the report. Empress Dowager Bo heard of this, and Emperor Xiaowen removed his cap and apologized, saying, “I have not taught my sons carefully.” Empress Dowager Bo then sent an envoy to convey an imperial pardon to the Crown Prince and the Prince of Liang, after which they were allowed to enter. Emperor Xiaowen thereupon regarded Shizhi as remarkable and appointed him as a Zhongdai Fu (Grand Master of the Middle).

Shortly after, he rose to the rank of Zhonglang Jiang. He accompanied the Emperor to Baling, where they stayed in the north, overlooking the latrines. At that time, Consort Shen was with them. The Emperor pointed to the Xinfeng Road and said to Consort Shen, “This is the road to Handan.” He made Consort Shen play the se (zither), and he himself leaned on the se and sang, his mood sad and melancholy. He looked back at the ministers and said, “Alas! If we use the stone of Beishan as a coffin, fill it with hemp and cotton, and seal it with lacquer, could it be moved?” Those around him all said, “Good.” Shizhi stepped forward and said, “If there is something desirable inside, even if sealed with the stone of Beishan, there will still be a crack. If there is nothing desirable inside, even without a stone coffin, what need for sorrow?” Emperor Xiaowen praised him as good. Later, he appointed Shizhi as Tingwei (Minister of Justice).

Shortly after, the Emperor went out and crossed the Zhongwei Bridge. A man came out from under the bridge, and the Emperor’s carriage horses were startled. The Emperor ordered cavalry to capture him and handed him over to the Tingwei. Shizhi investigated and questioned him. The man said, “I am a man of the county. I heard the warning drums, so I hid under the bridge. After a long time, I thought the procession had passed, so I came out. Seeing the Emperor’s carriage and cavalry, I ran away.” The Tingwei, Qin, judged that one person who violated the warning drums should be fined. Emperor Xiaowen was angry and said, “This man personally startled my horse. My horse happened to be gentle; if it had been another horse, would it not have injured me? Yet the Tingwei only judges him to be fined!” Shizhi said, “The law is what the Son of Heaven shares with the world in common. Now, if the law is thus but we make it heavier, the law will not be trusted by the people. Moreover, at that moment, if the Emperor had ordered him to be executed on the spot, that would have been done. Now that he has been handed to the Tingwei, the Tingwei is the standard of fairness for the whole world. If it tilts once, the application of law throughout the world will become arbitrary, and where will the people place their hands and feet? I beg Your Majesty to examine this.” After a long while, the Emperor said, “The Tingwei’s judgment is correct.”

Later, someone stole a jade ring from before the seat in the High Temple. He was captured, and Emperor Xiaowen was angry, handing him to the Tingwei for trial. Shizhi, according to the law, stated that stealing items used by the ancestral temple or the emperor was punishable by death in the marketplace. The Emperor was greatly angry and said, “How inhuman! To steal vessels from the late Emperor’s temple. I handed him to the Tingwei intending to have his whole clan executed, but you have judged him according to the law. This is not how I intend to honor and serve the ancestral temples.” Shizhi removed his cap, bowed his head to the ground, and apologized, saying, “The law is sufficient for this. Moreover, crimes are equal, but the penalty varies according to whether the act is rebellious or obedient. Now, if stealing temple vessels leads to the execution of the clan, what if, in a millionth of a chance, a foolish commoner takes a handful of earth from Changling? How would Your Majesty increase the penalty for him?” After a long time, Emperor Xiaowen discussed it with the Empress Dowager, and then allowed the Tingwei’s judgment. At that time, the Zhongwei, the Marquis of Tiao, Zhou Yafu, and the Liang Chancellor, the Marquis of Shandu, Wang Tiankai, saw that Shizhi held to fair judgments, and they became close friends with him. Zhang Tingwei was thus praised throughout the world.

Later, Emperor Xiaowen died, and Emperor Jing was established. Shizhi was afraid and claimed illness. He wished to resign, fearing a great punishment would come; he wished to meet and apologize, but did not know how it would go. He used the advice of Wang Sheng, and finally met and apologized. Emperor Jing did not hold it against him.

Wang Sheng was a recluse who was skilled in the teachings of Huang and Lao. He was once summoned to reside in the court. The Three Excellencies and Nine Ministers all gathered and stood. Wang Sheng was an old man and said, “My cap string is undone.” He looked at Zhang Tingwei and said, “Tie my cap string for me!” Shizhi knelt and tied it. Afterward, someone said to Wang Sheng, “Why did you humiliate Zhang Tingwei in the court, making him kneel to tie your cap string?” Wang Sheng said, “I am old and lowly, and I know I am of no benefit to Zhang Tingwei. Zhang Tingwei is currently a famous minister of the world. I deliberately humiliated the Tingwei, making him kneel to tie my cap string, to increase his prestige.” When the officials heard this, they considered Wang Sheng worthy and respected Zhang Tingwei.

Zhang Tingwei served Emperor Jing for more than a year, then became the Chancellor of the Prince of Huainan, still due to previous faults. After a long time, Shizhi died. His son was Zhang Zhi, courtesy name Changgong, who rose to the rank of Dafu (Grand Master) but was dismissed. Because he could not please the times, he did not hold office for the rest of his life.

Feng Tang’s great-grandfather was a man of Zhao. His father moved to Dai. When Han rose, he moved to Anling. Tang was noted for his filial piety and became the Chief of the Zhonglang Office, serving Emperor Xiaowen. When the Emperor passed by in his carriage, he asked Tang, “Old man, how did you become a Lang? Where is your home?” Tang answered truthfully. Emperor Xiaowen said, “When I was in Dai, my Supervisor of Food, Gao Qu, often spoke to me of the worthiness of the Zhao general Li Qi, who fought under Julu. Now, every time I eat, my thoughts are not absent from Julu. Do you know him?” Tang answered, “He is not as good as Lian Po or Li Mu as generals.” The Emperor said, “Why?” Tang said, “My great-grandfather, when in Zhao, was an officer and was on good terms with Li Mu. My father was formerly the Chancellor of Dai and was on good terms with the Zhao general Li Qi, knowing his character.” After the Emperor heard of the characters of Lian Po and Li Mu, he was greatly pleased and struck his thigh, saying, “Alas! If only I could have Lian Po and Li Mu as my generals in my time, would I worry about the Xiongnu?” Tang said, “My lord! Even if Your Majesty obtained Lian Po and Li Mu, you would not be able to use them.” The Emperor was angry, rose, and entered the inner palace. After a long while, he summoned Tang and rebuked him, saying, “How could you humiliate me in public? Was there no private place?” Tang apologized, saying, “I am a rustic and did not know the taboos.”

At that time, the Xiongnu had recently made a great incursion into Chaona, killing the Commandant of Beidi, Ang. The Emperor, concerned about the Hu invaders, finally asked Tang again, “How do you know that I cannot use Lian Po and Li Mu?” Tang answered, “I have heard that ancient kings, when dispatching generals, would kneel and push the carriage axle, saying: ‘Within the threshold, I decide; outside the threshold, the general decides.’ Military merits, ranks, and rewards were all decided outside, and reported upon return. This is not an empty statement. My great-grandfather said that Li Mu, as a Zhao general stationed at the border, used the taxes from the military market entirely to feast the soldiers. Rewards were decided outside, without interference from the center. He was entrusted with the task and held responsible for success. Therefore, Li Mu was able to fully use his intelligence and ability. He dispatched 1,300 selected chariots, 13,000 mounted archers, and 100,000 brave soldiers. Thus, he pursued the Chanyu to the north, broke the Eastern Hu, destroyed the Danlin, suppressed the strong Qin to the west, and supported Han and Wei to the south. At that time, Zhao was nearly hegemonic. Later, it happened that King Qian of Zhao was established, whose mother was a singer. When King Qian was established, he listened to the slander of Guo Kai and finally executed Li Mu, ordering Yan Ju to replace him. As a result, the army was broken, the soldiers fled, and Zhao was captured and destroyed by Qin. Now, I have secretly heard that Wei Shang is the Guardian of Yunzhong. He uses all the taxes from the military market to feast the soldiers and officers, and uses his private money to slaughter an ox every five days to feast guests, military officers, and retainers. Therefore, the Xiongnu keep far away and do not approach the Yunzhong frontier. When the enemy once entered, Shang led chariots and cavalry to attack them, killing many. The soldiers are all sons of common families, raised from the fields to join the army; how could they know the details of military regulations and unit symbols? They fight with all their strength all day, beheading and capturing enemies, and report their merits to the military office. If one word does not match, the clerks bind them with the law. Rewards are not carried out, but officials strictly apply the law. I, in my ignorance, think Your Majesty’s law is too clear, rewards too light, and punishments too heavy. Moreover, the Guardian of Yunzhong, Wei Shang, was judged for reporting six more heads than the actual number, and Your Majesty handed him to the officials, stripped him of his rank, and punished him with forced labor. From this, it can be said that even if Your Majesty obtained Lian Po and Li Mu, you would not be able to use them. I am truly foolish and have touched the taboos. It is a death crime, a death crime!” Emperor Xiaowen was pleased. That day, he ordered Feng Tang to hold the imperial seal and pardon Wei Shang, restoring him as the Guardian of Yunzhong, and appointed Tang as the Commandant of Chariots and Cavalry, in charge of the Zhongwei and the chariot and cavalry troops of the commanderies and kingdoms.

In the seventh year, Emperor Jing was established, and Tang was made Chancellor of Chu, but was dismissed. When Emperor Wu was established, he sought worthy and good men, and Feng Tang was recommended. Tang was then over ninety years old and could no longer serve as an official, so his son, Feng Sui, was made a Lang. Sui’s courtesy name was Wangsun, and he was also a remarkable man, and was on good terms with me.

The Grand Historian says: Zhang Ji’s words show a worthy man who upholds the law and does not flatter; Feng Gong’s discussion of generals is full of flavor! Full of flavor! The saying goes, “If you do not know a man, look at his friends.” The men praised by these two gentlemen can be recorded in the corridors and temples. The Book of Documents says, “Not partial, not partisan, the royal way is vast; not partisan, not partial, the royal way is broad.” Zhang Ji and Feng Gong are close to this. [end of text]


